# Writing Stakeholder Emails — Method

> An email is an **instrument to produce a decision**, not a report of what you know.
> Proportions matter: ~80% thinking & verification, ~20% writing.

## Steps (in order)

### 1. Start from the reply you want
Before writing anything, finish this sentence: *"After reading this, they will reply with ___."*
If you can't, you're not ready to write. The desired reply is the design constraint for everything else.

### 2. Make the ask answerable, not just explicit
Open-ended asks ("tell me your priorities") get postponed forever. Bounded asks ("your top 3, and the decision each supports") get answered.
When asking people to prioritize, give them the cost: tag each option as *structural / needs instrumentation / modeling work on existing data*. A menu without prices makes everyone order the lobster.

### 3. Find the one causal frame that explains everything
A list of facts that looks arbitrary reads as excuses. Hunt for the single root cause behind the pattern (e.g. *"source A knows the users but not the money; source B knows the money but not the users"*).
One organizing principle up front beats ten scattered justifications — it transfers understanding, not just conclusions. If you can't find one, your own understanding is probably incomplete.

### 4. Run the collision check
For every number or claim: *what will the reader compare it against, and will the comparison break?*
A pre-empted contradiction costs one ⚠️ line; a discovered one costs your credibility on every other line.
**A ✅ with a hidden caveat is more expensive than a ⚠️.** Trust is the actual deliverable; the content is just the vehicle.

### 5. Verify against the record, not your recall
Memory compresses: headlines survive, caveats and root causes die — and the caveats are where the trust lives.
Before writing, open the source of truth (facts page, past tickets, previous analyses) and diff the audience's *past asks* against your outline. Anything unverified gets flagged or checked — never silently promoted to ✅.

### 6. Structure last, for the skimmer
Only now open the compose window. Pyramid principle:
- Purpose in the first two sentences
- Body grouped by the **reader's** categories, not your data sources
- The ask isolated at the end, where a skimmer lands
- Assume the key reader spends 40 seconds; bold the load-bearing phrases
- Write it forwardable: it will be read by people you didn't send it to, with zero context

## Supporting system

Maintain a **per-domain facts page** (findings, root causes, caveats, date established, open verifications) and update it when closing each analysis. Then "how I start" becomes mechanical:

1. Write the one-line decision the email must produce
2. Open the facts page — that's your draft material, not your memory
3. Diff their past asks against your outline
4. Only then start writing

Don't trust your memory — build systems instead.

## Pre-send checklist

- [ ] Can I state the reply I expect, in one sentence?
- [ ] Is the ask bounded and easy to answer?
- [ ] Does one causal frame make the limitations self-explanatory?
- [ ] Does every number survive comparison with the figures the reader already knows?
- [ ] Did I check the record (past asks, established findings) rather than my recall?
- [ ] Does every ✅ deserve it — no hidden caveats?
- [ ] Would a 40-second skimmer still catch the purpose and the ask?
- [ ] Does it survive being forwarded without context?

